{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1366 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "af28f4dba7e1eba67f1aaf4a4d229bf6d7b83aa27ce31ce16c0b0a7c7646caef" :edb/db-before-t 1365 :edb/db-after-t 1366 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534678 50 #inst "2026-10-12T07:19:40.461Z" 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186194453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194454 13194139534678 17592186045417 false] [17592186194453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194455 13194139534678 17592186045417 false] [17592186194453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194456 13194139534678 17592186045417 false] [17592186194453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194457 13194139534678 17592186045417 false] [17592186194453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194458 13194139534678 17592186045417 false] [17592186194453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432346 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186194453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432348 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186194453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432356 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186194453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432358 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186194453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432359 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186194453 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432358 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198077 1071 "For the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf\\left(x,y \\right) \n=\nx^2y-xy^3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/0d/0de380d24ed675cff86b219a2a4a337699d68e168fc875475158523022915b0c.svg) Give your answer in matrix form." 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198077 1071 "For the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf\\left(x,y \\right) \n=\nx^2y-xy^3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/b7/b799b1e1fbe7819de86c131989530033d750669ddc8fee88c711b537dad982a6.png) Give your answer in matrix form." 13194139534678 17592186045417 false] [17592186198077 1074 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/61/61c5a4204c4ad078bd036eee7770e28faf4741bedb274a54d08d54dae43fbc68.svg) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/ab/ab531b6a5468a0ddb8cf46a42300f19cc6406c94c011ae814d60af24f66e2aa0.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/3d/3dbb0d517f6875d4a0601ba284878cb60ac0b615491564ae9458c34c0f3de403.svg)\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-{y}^{3} & {x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by\n\n![](images/3c/3cbbec95c6327e77a53bdeea5de4a93481ca9f4164ebae0cb99bf28468513a12.svg)" 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198077 1074 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/b1/b1c2d4408c64daf16466d81c1ea97bdd32f821deb858cd09bc539fc53ebf3ad8.png) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/c5/c54318fa746f53209585695e2c40d80d793d2b35327b257426b4e4fa7b25463b.png)\n\nand\n\n![](images/95/952dafd201f20f1da5b0ac77527beeb4f6d7c259813dbaee74a8d6796dd5d3b2.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-{y}^{3} & {x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by\n\n![](images/fc/fc105c91cf998f6b564570697321cfb72ac30f81e88dd6c1a0decfe8495259ba.png)" 13194139534678 17592186045417 false] [17592186198078 1071 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/31/31305b4a4a27141bae70eabbb04acaf98928c275f8efec37ef8a869c7599a2f3.svg) to approximate the value of $f(1,1,1).$" 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198078 1071 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/32/32af7a7ab867c247eff15583688e31052e2d640b1be6c6c1b1f8bd19b7b7f2f7.png) to approximate the value of $f(1,1,1).$" 13194139534678 17592186045417 false] [17592186198078 1074 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/a7/a7a5f8961c77ad8a606ce57a543d5606b834061f3a0777b074a5711dcc9a4529.svg) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/27/276fd591c58ea0bd80eb0d3c236ade53fcd1d38864ccd650873cf052561b7586.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/06/06c98e6248f7fdb303c04772921b0423f8e2f88450d330c295bc3a72846ef858.svg)\n\nIn this case, we have ![](images/3a/3ac30d1603a502de1404a1888f7c410d22ae93b8b47db6694734a09634a43954.svg) and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n![](images/af/afd14fa251e88edb40ea71b70c56db5a03ec83d906608930e2b3ba3ba9c9fb0b.svg)\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at ![](images/1a/1af733b173aa4a358cd8c0b5c2754431be57887e7c43b0aa98125deefdecc4ad.svg) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198078 1074 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/f2/f27bcd29776525aa543b778627e0aace4e0b0537ceac83f5750a31f5def4f762.png) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/c2/c281563d780c34ea5753498e0e722bfb0296fe06c5d43e82473a45901a1aa7ee.png)\n\nand\n\n![](images/2f/2fa9abbaad8230a38e1085b8842fbbd970c66addff6f388e13973ee116f0cd37.png)\n\nIn this case, we have ![](images/71/71175744ceb56efc01b8b18b97d6681b11b8e9b85a1d899fc9a137ab5e3c611c.png) and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n![](images/0d/0d3a22cd82a02ab5122e811236d53eb4c0018e9817d2542163acc005ca5d3764.png)\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at ![](images/da/dad24b303457c56dc14c11b3b9de6cb827b18b8f3a03059922d1b15521ea119d.png) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534678 17592186045417 false] [17592186198434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432370 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432374 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432372 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432371 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432373 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198498 1050 1260.0 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198637 1071 "For the function $f: R^{2} → R$, defined as\n\n$f(x, y) = x^{3}y + xy^{3} + y^{2}$,\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$f(x, y) \\approx f(a) + f^{′}(a)(x - a) + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & ∗ \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." 13194139534678 17592186045417 false] [17592186198637 1071 "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}+{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & ∗ \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186198637 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y+{y}^{3} & {x}^{3}+3x{y}^{2}+2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}6xy & 3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & 6xy+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-1)+3+(x-1)=2x+1.\n$$" 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186199501 1064 "Recall that the quadratic Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}.\n$$\n\nWe can extend the concept of Taylor polynomials to multivariable functions. \n\nGiven a twice-differentiable function $f(x,y),$ the **quadratic Taylor polynomial** of $f$ about the point $(a,b)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \\approx \\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about the origin $O$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \\approx f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLater in the lesson, we will develop some intuition for why the quadratic part looks the way it does. But before we do that, let's get some practice working with this formula." 13194139534678 17592186045417 false] [17592186199501 1064 "Recall that the quadratic Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx  \\overbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}^{\\text{Linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{Quadratic part}}.\n$$\n\nWe can extend the concept of Taylor polynomials to multivariable functions. \n\nGiven a twice-differentiable function $f(x,y),$ the **quadratic Taylor polynomial** of $f$ about the point $(a,b)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about the origin $O$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLater in the lesson, we will develop some intuition for why the quadratic part looks the way it does. But before we do that, let's get some practice working with this formula." 13194139534678 17592186045417 true] [17592186199502 1064 "It's possible to derive Taylor polynomials for functions of more than two variables. However, increasing the number of variables significantly increases the number of terms in the Taylor polynomial. \n\nIdeally, we'd like a compact expression for the quadratic Taylor polynomial that works for all twice-differentiable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$ This can be achieved using matrix notation. \n\nTo see how, let's consider the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f(x,y)$ about $(a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \\approx \\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ then the total derivative of $f(x,y)=f(\\mathbf{x})$ at $(a,b)=\\mathbf{a}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we can rewrite the linear part of the approximation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Linear approximation} & =f(a,b)+{f}_{x}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)+{f}_{y}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b) \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =f(a)+[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(a,b) & {f}_{y}(a,b)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =f(a)+{f}^{'}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is just our usual linear approximation of a function $f.$\n\nThe second derivative of $f$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $f_{xy} = f_{yx},$ we can rewrite the quadratic part of the approximation using matrix multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Quadratic part} & =\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(a,b)\\cdot (x-a)(y-b)+{f}_{yy}(a,b)\\cdot (y-b)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}x-a & y-b\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-a \\\\ y-b\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}x & y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a & b\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(a) & {f}_{xy}(a) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(a) & {f}_{yy}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{''}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our quadratic Taylor polynomial can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & \\approx \\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(a)+{f}^{'}(a)(x-a)}}+{{\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f